From the source, trap.c:

if (Luck > -13 && rn2(13+Luck) > 7) {  /* saved by luck */

The comment is accurate; with max luck (13), roughly 3/4 of the time a
chest trap will not even go off properly.  With zero luck, even 39% of
traps will still be duds.

Exploding chests that are "duds" because of good luck do not destroy
their contents.  Chests are generated with traps 1 in 10 times.  Also,
consider the following code, which is used to select which trap occurs
for the times you actually may have set off a trap:

switch(rn2(20) ? ((Luck >= 13) ? 0 : rn2(13-Luck)) : rn2(26)) {

This is typical Nethack incomprehensible style, of course; chained
ternary operators deserve a beating.  In plain english, though...

-- A d20 is rolled.
-- If d20 is not zero and luck is maxed, set trap # to "0".
-- If luck is not maxed, set trap number to somewhere between 
	0 and (13 - Luck); for a zero-luck player, that's 0-12.
		(We will ignore the negative-luck cases here because you're going to
		have so much else go wrong with negative luck, it should be something
		you fix immediately).
-- If the d20 _is_ zero (5% chance), set trap number to somewhere
	between 0 and 25.

Traps are numbered in the following sequence:

25-21: Explosion trap (destroys chest and contents)
20-17: Poison gas cloud.
16-13: Poison needle.
 12-9: Fire trap.
  8-6: Shock trap (blows up worn rings)
  5-3: Paralysis trap.
  2-0: Hallucination cloud.

So let's review, shall we?  To have an explosion trap destroy the Castle
wand, the following has to happen for a max-luck character:

-- Must hit 10% chance of castle-wand chest being trapped.
-- Must hit 25% chance of trap even going off.
-- Must hit 5% chance of getting a "bad" trap.
-- Must hit 19% chance of getting the explosion trap.

For a zero-luck character, the only thing that changes is the first
number; it's now a 61% chance of the trap actually having an effect.

What does all this mean?  Your described scenario has a 0.023% chance of
happening for a maxluck player; it has a 0.057% chance of happening for a
normal-luck player.

In plainer English, that is one-fiftieth of one percent for maxluck, and
one-twentieth of one percent for zero-luck.

In even plainer English: _You should play your game as though the
possibility of this scenario happening were 0%_.

> Therefore, I'm prevently do #untrap at least twice on each chest, an 
> probably 5 to ten on the castle one... 

The numbers say you're wasting your time.  Caution is key in Nethack.
Being cautious about too many things, though, leads to "forest for the 
trees" syndrome, where you're so focused on something that's incredibly
unlikely to happen that you make mistakes in other aspects.

